window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"10-15","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"10-15-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-15-01/","title_he":"א – פתיחה לברכות הראייה","title_en":"Introduction to the Blessings over Sights","body_en":"<p>Every day we praise and thank God for the wonderful world He created for our sake – in Birkot Ha-shaḥar, in the blessings of Keri'at Shema and in the prayers. However, in addition to the order of the fixed prayers and blessings, we sometimes encounter special, moving and stirring sights, and in order to express their value-laden content, the Sages instituted the recitation of a blessing upon seeing them, thereby connecting them to their root in faith. Therefore, they instituted blessings upon seeing special landscapes, natural phenomena, places where miracles occurred, new settlements in the Land of Israel, sages and kings, beautiful and unusual creatures, as well as sights that shake the soul, such as idolatry or graves.</p><p>The Sages instituted these blessings as an obligation. However, when there is doubt regarding a particular sight, whether it is among the things over which they instituted a blessing, because of the doubt one does not recite the blessing, for in cases of doubt regarding blessings we are lenient. One who wishes to be meticulous may say the blessing without God's name and kingship.</p><p>In order to recite the blessings over sights, two conditions must be fulfilled: first, that the sight be special and arouse wonder in most people; second, that the viewer not have seen it for thirty days, for then his seeing it is something new for him (SA 218:3; 224:13). And although there are sensitive people who would be moved by the sight even after a week, and there are indifferent people who would not be moved even after a year, the Sages instituted the blessing according to what is common among most people, for whom, after thirty days, a sense of newness is aroused in their souls.</p><p>To make it easier to count the days, let us explain that the thirty-first day, on which one blesses a second time, will always come four weeks and two days later. Thus, if one saw it on Sunday, the thirty-first day will arrive four weeks later on Tuesday. And if one saw it on Monday, the thirty-first day will arrive four weeks later on Wednesday.</p><p>The blessing must be recited during the time of seeing, or at the latest within toch kedei dibbur (the time it takes to say a few words) of the end of the seeing (see SA 227:3; BHL 218:1, s.v. \"be-makom\").</p><p>When several people are together and become obligated in the same blessing, it is proper for each one to bless for himself. And when there are some who do not know how to recite the blessing or do not remember its wording, it is better that one person recite the blessing for them (above 12:7). It seems that if they set out on a joint excursion, they have become one group that sees the sights together, and therefore one may recite the blessing aloud on behalf of all of them, and all who hear respond \"Amen\" after him (see SA 213:1).</p><p>One who sees these sights through eyeglasses, his seeing is considered ordinary seeing. But one who sees these sights in a picture or a film, or with the help of a telescope, does not recite the blessing, for such seeing does not arouse wonder as tangible seeing does. It is a fact that people are not satisfied with seeing pictures and films, but go out on trips in order to see things with their own eyes.</p><p>From what distance may one recite the blessing? From a distance at which one can be impressed by the special sight. Over a mountain or a sea one may recite the blessing from a great distance. In order to bless over a hill, one must come closer, so as to discern its unique shape. In order to bless over a sage or a king, one must come closer still, so as to discern his figure. And in order to bless over a beautiful creature, one must come closer still, so as to discern its beauty.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-15-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-15-02/","title_he":"ב – ברכת ימים, נהרות, מדבריות, הרים וגבעות","title_en":"The Blessing over Seas, Rivers, Deserts, Mountains and Hills","body_en":"<p>Through seeing impressive landscapes, one can contemplate the greatness of the Creator and say a blessing of praise before Him. Therefore, the Sages instituted that one who sees seas, rivers, mountains, hills and deserts recites: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who performs the work of creation\" (<i>oseh ma'aseh bereishit</i>) (Berakhot 54a). Even one who is not moved by seeing them, as long as seeing them is considered moving in the eyes of most people and thirty days have passed for him without seeing them – is obligated to recite the blessing.</p><p>A <b>sea</b> is always impressive, and therefore one recites the blessing over every sea and lake, provided that it contains much water all year round and is not man-made. Therefore, one recites the blessing upon seeing the Mediterranean Sea and the Red Sea, and one likewise recites the blessing over the Sea of Galilee (Kinneret) and the Dead Sea, which have water all year round. But if the lake was formed by a dam that people built, even if it is very large, one does not recite the blessing over it, since the blessing was instituted as praise for the work of the Creator and not for the works of human beings.</p><p>Over the Great Sea, which is the ocean that surrounds the continents, one recites: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who made the Great Sea\" (<i>she-asa et ha-yam ha-gadol</i>). Admittedly, some maintain that the Great Sea is the Mediterranean Sea, which is called great because of its eminence in being near the Land of Israel, or because even seas of its size are considered great (Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona; SA 228:1). However, according to many poskim, only over the ocean does one recite \"who made the Great Sea,\" whereas over the Mediterranean Sea one recites \"who performs the work of creation.\" And this is the practice (Rosh; Ra'av; MA; MB 228:2; see BHL there).</p><p>Regarding <b>rivers</b>, two conditions were stated: <b>a)</b> that they flow naturally, without human beings having changed the course of their flow; <b>b)</b> that they be at least as large as the Euphrates, which is called \"great\" in the Torah. All the more so does one recite the blessing over larger rivers such as the Nile, the Volga, the Rhine, the Amazon and the Mississippi. But over ordinary rivers such as the Yarkon, the Jordan and the like, one does not recite the blessing, for they are not so impressive (MB 228:2).</p><p><b>Mountains and hills</b>: regarding mountains, the condition is that they be especially high relative to their surroundings, and regarding hills, the condition is that their shape be especially impressive, for example that they have steep and jagged cliffs, such as the impressive cliffs in the Judean Desert. But over the ordinary mountains in the mountains of Judea, Samaria and the Galilee – one should not recite the blessing. Upon seeing Gamla, Arbel, Masada and Sartaba, one recites the blessing, because their appearance is special. One likewise recites the blessing over Mount Tabor, whose height is impressive, and which has an appearance … [?].<sup>1</sup></p><p>A <b>desert</b> is an arid and desolate place, where little rain falls. One recites the blessing over the Judean Desert as well, on condition that the sight makes an impression, for example when one goes hiking within it and all the surrounding areas are desolate, or goes to a lookout point in order to gaze at its arid expanses. But one who sees it while traveling routinely on the road – does not recite the blessing.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"Over impressive mountains that hikers customarily travel to see, one certainly must recite the blessing. Examples of this: in the Golan – Mount Hermon and Gamla. In the Galilee – Arbel, Mount Ḥazon, Mount Atzmon, Mount Meron, the Rosh Ha-nikra ridge (on its western side). In Samaria and Judea – Mounts Gerizim and Eival, Mount Kabir, Mount Tamun, Sartaba, Mount Ba'al Ḥatzor, Masada, the Fault Escarpment along its entire length, at a lookout over the Dead Sea valley. In the Jezreel Valley – Mount Tabor and the Gilboa (mainly Mount Shaul). In the Carmel – Mount Carmel, in the places where it descends steeply to the valley or to the sea. In the Negev and Eilat – the makhteshim (erosion craters) (looking into the makhtesh), Mount Ardon (in Makhtesh Ramon), Mount Shlomo, Mount Tzfaḥot. These are examples of well-known mountains, but anyone who is impressed by similar ones must recite the blessing."}]},{"slug":"10-15-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-15-03/","title_he":"ג – מספר ברכות על נופים שונים","title_en":"Several Blessings over Various Landscapes","body_en":"<p>Seeing one great mountain does not affect the seeing of another mountain, so that if one sees the Hermon and immediately afterward flies to Europe and sees the Alps, he recites \"who performs the work of creation\" again. Only over seeing the same mountain within thirty days does one not recite the blessing.</p><p>It emerges that a person may become obligated in the blessing \"who performs the work of creation\" several times in one day: if in one day he sees the Mediterranean Sea, and afterward Mount Hermon, and afterward Lake Kinneret, and afterward the Dead Sea, and afterward the Red Sea, he recites the blessing over each of them separately. Likewise, one who flies from the Land of Israel to America, when he sees the Mediterranean Sea recites \"who performs the work of creation,\" and after he passes Europe and sees the Atlantic Ocean, he recites \"who made the Great Sea.\" And if he sees especially great mountains from the airplane – he recites \"who performs the work of creation\" over them as well.</p><p>One who sees together several landscapes over which a blessing must be recited recites one blessing over all of them. For example, one who is in a place from which he sees the Kinneret and Mount Arbel recites one blessing over both of them.</p><p>One who hikes in an area in which there are several special hills of similar shape, since they all belong to the same area and there is a resemblance in their shape, even if he sees them one after another, recites one blessing over all of them. Likewise regarding great mountains: as long as they are in one area, he recites one blessing over all of them. For example, the entire Carmel is considered one area. Judea, Samaria and the Galilee, which are larger territories, are divided into several areas according to the terrain. And he should recite the blessing over the mountains separately and over the hills separately, unless he sees them together.</p><p>One who hikes in the Judean Desert, when he enters the desert, recites the blessing over the desert, and if afterward he sees an especially great mountain – he recites the blessing over it as well. And when he reaches the place of the impressive cliffs, he recites the blessing over them as well, and even if he later sees more special cliffs there, the blessing he recited at first covers all of them, since they are in the same area and their shape is similar.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-15-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-15-04/","title_he":"ד – ראייה שיש בה חידוש והתפעלות","title_en":"Seeing That Involves Novelty and Wonder","body_en":"<p>One who lives near the sea or regularly travels near it, since seeing it holds nothing new for him – does not recite the blessing; but upon seeing a different sea – he recites the blessing. Therefore, one who lives close to the Mediterranean coast, or regularly travels on the coastal highway, does not recite the blessing upon seeing the Mediterranean Sea, even if he sees it from another shore. And even if by chance thirty days pass in which he did not look at it – he should not recite the blessing, for since he could easily have gazed at it, seeing it holds nothing new for him. But if he leaves his place for thirty days, and upon returning wishes to gaze at the sea, he is considered one who \"sees\" and recites the blessing. The same law applies to those who live near a high mountain or a special hill.<sup>2</sup></p><p>One who is far from the special landscape, or for whom clouds blur its appearance, since such seeing does not arouse wonder, should not recite the blessing over it. And if on the next day he comes close to it and sees it well, his seeing will be considered a new seeing and he will be obligated to recite the blessing over it.</p><p>It is not possible to fix distances for this, for everything depends on the size of the landscape and the angle of view. The rule is that when the sight is impressive – one recites the blessing.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"The basis for this is the law regarding one who lives near places of idolatry, who does not recite the blessing upon seeing it, since it is not new to him (Tosafot 57b; Nimukei Yosef there; Hagahot Maimoniyot on Rambam, Hilkhot Berakhot 10:9). According to the Olat Tamid, their intent is to say that since thirty days do not pass without his seeing the idolatry – he should not recite the blessing. However, according to the Eliya Rabba, even if by chance he did not see the idolatry for thirty days, he should not recite the blessing, for those who live there, seeing it holds nothing new. So too wrote the Nahar Shalom, Pri Megadim, and MB 224:3. And from this we learn for all the blessings over sights. However, there is a doubt regarding one who lives somewhat far from the sea, such that naturally he sees the sea only once every few months – whether he is among those who can easily see the sea. In practice, if he indeed does not see the sea for thirty days, and is moved by its appearance – he recites the blessing, for perhaps he is not considered one of those who live near it, and even if he is considered one of those who live near it, the opinion of the Olat Tamid and those who agree with him is that whenever one has not seen it for thirty days – he recites the blessing. But if he is not moved – he should not recite the blessing."}]},{"slug":"10-15-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-15-05/","title_he":"ה – ברכת ראיית הנופים לימינו","title_en":"The Blessing over Seeing Landscapes in Our Times","body_en":"<p>In our times, when people are accustomed to covering great distances by car in order to reach their work or social and family gatherings, a person often sees mountains, hills and seas while traveling, and the question arises whether he should recite the blessing over such seeing as well, which occurs incidentally and routinely. The basis of the doubt is that in the past, when people went on foot or on a donkey, they only rarely saw the special landscapes, and therefore when they saw the sea, they were moved by the sight of it, and when they walked along the shore and suddenly saw the lofty Carmel mountains touching the shore, they were moved by seeing them. But today, people are accustomed to traveling there back and forth, and the sight has become routine. Moreover, in the past, when people went on foot and on donkeys, they were accustomed to seeing fewer mountains, and therefore Mount Tabor and the Carmel were considered in their eyes to be extremely moving mountains. But today, when people are accustomed to traveling in many countries, and have already seen mountains higher and loftier than these, the Carmel and Mount Tabor may seem to them average mountains that do not make a special impression.</p><p>In practice, only one who pays attention to the special landscapes is considered one who \"sees\" and must recite the blessing. But one before whose eyes the landscapes passed without his contemplating them – should not recite the blessing. Therefore, one must distinguish between two kinds of seeing: during a trip and during routine travel. During a trip, since the purpose of the trip is to contemplate the beauty of creation, it is clear that one must recite the blessing over all the special landscapes, including the sea, Mount Tabor and the Carmel, provided that one has not seen them for thirty days and does not live near them or regularly travel near them (as explained in the previous halakha). Even one who personally is not impressed, since he went out on a trip in order to see the landscapes, regards them as significant and is obligated to recite the blessing. And if he is in doubt whether the sights he sees are impressive enough, it is proper that he say the blessing without God's name and kingship. And if hikers customarily come to see them, this is a sign that they are impressive, and one must recite the blessing over them without doubt.</p><p>However, during routine travel, the law depends on wonder and emotion. If the sight catches his attention – he recites the blessing. And if it does not catch his attention, even though he sees it – he should not recite the blessing. For example, one who travels from Jerusalem to Haifa by way of the coastal highway, if he pays attention to the sea and is somewhat moved – he recites the blessing. And if not – he should not recite the blessing. If he pays attention to the Carmel mountains and contemplates their special appearance and is somewhat moved – he recites the blessing, and if not – he should not recite the blessing. And so too regarding Mount Tabor, the Kinneret and the lofty mountains of Judea and Samaria.<sup>3</sup></p><p>One who traveled near a sea or near a great mountain and did not pay attention to its sight, and therefore also did not recite the blessing, and within thirty days traveled again and contemplated and was impressed by the sight – should not recite the blessing. Even though at the time of the previous seeing he was not moved and did not recite the blessing, it is nevertheless considered a seeing that causes anything he sees within thirty days not to be considered a new seeing. For the fact that he did not recite the blessing the previous time was not because he did not see, but because he was not impressed by what he saw. But if the first seeing was from afar or from an angle that is not impressive – he recites the blessing over the second seeing.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"On the one hand, one cannot say that since the Sages instituted the blessing over special mountains and hills according to what is accepted in the Land of Israel, every viewer is obligated to recite the blessing over them, for we have already learned in the previous note that one does not recite the blessing over a seeing that does not arouse wonder among many of the local people. Therefore, whoever is not impressed, as many travelers are not impressed, should not recite the blessing. On the other hand, one cannot say that since many are not impressed, even one who is impressed should not recite the blessing, because the essence of the Sages' enactment to recite the blessing over them remains in place"}]},{"slug":"10-15-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-15-06/","title_he":"ו – רעידת אדמה, כוכבי שביט, רוחות זועפות","title_en":"Earthquakes, Comets and Raging Winds","body_en":"<p>Usually we encounter orderly nature – the sun rises and sets in its order, the stars move in their courses, and everything stands firmly in place, and for this we thank God in the blessing of Yotzer Ha-me'orot and in Pesukei De-zimra. However, at times we encounter extraordinary phenomena that shake the soul, to the point that it seems as though the orders that the Creator implanted in His world no longer hold. But we must remember that everything is from God, and even the exceptional and shocking phenomena are His works and His mighty deeds, intended to straighten the world and to repair it, as the Sages said (Berakhot 59a): \"Thunder was created only to straighten the crookedness of the heart.\"</p><p>The Sages mentioned five wondrous and tumultuous phenomena: a) one who feels an earthquake; b) one who sees a comet flying across the sky, and likewise one who sees a falling star, which is a meteor that meets the atmosphere and burns up until its light flashes brightly; c) over raging storm winds; d) one who sees lightning; e) one who hears thunder (SA 227:1).</p><p>And what does one recite? One of two blessings: either \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, whose power and might fill the world\" (<i>she-koḥo u-gevurato malei olam</i>), or \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who performs the work of creation.\" Ideally, it is preferable to recite \"whose power and might\" over the frightening events, such as a terrible storm, a strong earthquake and thunder; and over the less frightening ones, such as stars and lightning – \"the work of creation\" (see MB 227:4-5).</p><p>One who feels an <b>earthquake</b>, even a light one – recites the blessing. And if close to this he feels another tremor, with the blessing he recited at first he has also covered the subsequent tremor. But if he had already diverted his attention and then felt an earthquake again, he recites the blessing a second time (Birkei Yosef 227:3). And although we learned regarding the blessings over sights that one recites the blessing over the same sight only after thirty days, here we are dealing with a different earthquake, and it is like a different sight.</p><p>One who sees a comet (literally, a \"<b>star</b>\") and recited the blessing upon seeing it, and on the following night saw it again, should not recite the blessing over it, since thirty days have not passed since he saw it. And if on the following night he sees a different comet – he recites the blessing over it. And if in one night he sees two comets, or a comet and a falling star, he should not recite an additional blessing over the second, because there is an opinion that one blessing covers all the stars a person sees in one night (MB 227:1-2).</p><p><b>Storm winds</b>: over especially strong and raging winds, such as a tornado or hurricane, which have the power to uproot trees and destroy dilapidated houses, one recites \"whose power and might,\" and if one recited \"the work of creation\" – he has fulfilled his obligation. Over a less raging wind, but still strong enough to blow heavy things out of their place, one recites only \"the work of creation\" (MA 227:1, based on the Yerushalmi and Tosafot). As long as it is the same storm, one should not recite the blessing over it twice. But if the storm ended and after some time a different storm began, he recites the blessing again.</p><p>Over every special natural phenomenon, such as an erupting volcano, a geyser, impressive waterfalls and a solar or lunar eclipse, one must recite the blessing. For what the Sages enumerated are only examples, to teach us that anyone who sees an impressive natural phenomenon like them recites the blessing (see Sha'ar Ha-ayin 7, note 15; Or Le-Tziyon vol. 2, 46:63).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-15-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-15-07/","title_he":"ז – ברקים ורעמים","title_en":"Lightning and Thunder","body_en":"<p>Since one must recite a blessing over both lightning and thunder, and over both of them one may recite either of the two blessings, the custom is to recite first, upon seeing the lightning, \"who performs the work of creation,\" and afterward, upon hearing the thunder, whose sound is more frightening: \"whose power and might fill the world.\"</p><p>One who has already recited the blessing once during the day upon seeing lightning and hearing thunder should not recite the blessing over them again that same day. And although each lightning bolt and thunderclap is formed by itself, since they were formed from the same cloud system, one blessing covers all the lightning and one blessing all the thunder. But on the next day, even if it is still the same storm, he recites the blessing over them again. And even on the same day, if the clouds dispersed completely, and the sky was again covered with clouds and lightning and thunder began, since it is a different cloud system, he recites the blessing over them anew (SA 227:2; MB 8).</p><p>The blessings over lightning and thunder must be recited close to seeing or hearing them, that is, within toch kedei dibbur (the time it takes to say a few words), which is the time in which three words can be said. If one waited longer, he has lost the blessing, and he should wait in case he sees another flash of lightning, and then hurry to recite the blessing within toch kedei dibbur. And likewise regarding hearing thunder (SA 227:3; MB 12).</p><p>Similarly, the blessing over a comet, an earthquake or a storm wind must be recited during the event or immediately after it, and if the time of toch kedei dibbur has passed from the end of the event, he has lost the blessing.<sup>4</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"If one saw lightning or heard thunder while saying the blessings of Keri'at Shema, if he is in the middle of a blessing or in the middle of one of the paragraphs of Keri'at Shema, he should not interrupt in order to recite the blessing over them. But if he is between one blessing and the next or between one paragraph and the next, since this is a passing mitzva, for perhaps they will not appear again, he may interrupt and recite the blessing over them. And if he saw lightning or heard thunder in the middle of Pesukei De-zimra, he may interrupt and recite the blessing even in the middle of a psalm. The same law applies to an earthquake, seeing a comet and the like (see vol. 1, 20:3; MB 66:19)."}]},{"slug":"10-15-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-15-08/","title_he":"ח – ברכת האילנות בימי ניסן","title_en":"The Blessing over Trees in the Days of Nisan","body_en":"<p>One who goes out to a garden or a field in the month of Nisan and sees blossoming trees recites Birkat Ha-ilanot (the blessing over trees), in order to thank God for His kindness in reviving and making blossom the trees that stood dry in the winter.</p><p>The wording of the blessing: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who has left nothing lacking in His world, and created in it good creatures and good trees for human beings to enjoy\" (Berakhot 43b).<sup>5</sup></p><p>The blessing is over fruit trees only, from whose blossoms blessing grows, for they will develop into fruit. The Sages instituted the blessing over trees and not over vegetables because they are more beautiful. Furthermore, in trees the kindness of the Holy One, blessed be He, is more evident, for they produce fruit without toil. Furthermore, the basic food for a person's sustenance comes from the species of grain, legumes and other vegetables, and God did us a kindness and added for us the fruits of the tree, to vary our food with rich and choice flavors. Therefore we say in the blessing \"who has left nothing lacking in His world,\" even things that are not needed for survival.</p><p>Ideally, the blessing should be said upon seeing at least two trees, but when necessary one may recite the blessing even over one blossoming tree.</p><p>One who did not manage to see the trees in their blossoming, and saw them after their fruit had begun to grow, should not recite the blessing over them. However, if their fruit is tiny and barely noticeable, one may still recite the blessing over them after the fact (be-di'avad) (see MB 226:4; Birkat Hashem vol. 4, 4:121). If one saw the trees and did not recite the blessing, he has not lost the blessing, and he should recite it when he sees them again (MB 226:5).</p><p>The Sages said that the time of the blessing is in the days of Nisan, because that is when trees usually blossom, but according to the letter of the law, one may say the blessing before then or after then as well. The kabbalists greatly extolled the virtue of this blessing, for through it rectifications (<i>tikkunim</i>) are made for souls that have been reincarnated in trees, and these rectifications are made specifically in the month of Nisan. Therefore several Aḥaronim wrote that one must be careful to say the blessing specifically in the month of Nisan. The zealous hasten to recite the blessing on Rosh Ḥodesh Nisan. However, in practice, one who did not manage to say the blessing in the month of Nisan, according to most poskim, may make it up in the month of Iyar (Maḥatzit Ha-shekel OḤ 226; Yad Yitzḥak 1:198; Yismaḥ Lev OḤ 12).</p><p>In northern countries, where blossoming is delayed until the month of Iyar, one recites the blessing ideally in the month of Iyar. And one who is in the southern hemisphere, where the blossoming of trees occurs in the month of Tishrei, should recite the blessing over the trees in the month of Tishrei (Har Tzvi 1:118).</p><p>Some have the custom not to recite this blessing on Shabbat (Kaf Ha-ḥayim 226:4); however, according to most poskim, there is no reason to refrain from Birkat Ha-ilanot on Shabbat (Yeḥaveh Da'at 1:2). And this is the custom in many places.</p><p>One does not say the blessing over trees that are known to have been grafted in a forbidden manner, for according to many poskim, one should not praise God over a tree with which a transgression was committed (Halakhot Ketanot vol. 1, 60:265). Likewise, one should not say the blessing over orla trees (trees in their first three years, whose fruit is forbidden), for since it is forbidden to derive benefit from their fruit, it is not fitting to recite the blessing over them (R. Akiva Eiger). But whenever it is not known that the tree is grafted or orla, one may recite the blessing over it.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"The Sephardic wording (Siddur of Rav Eliyahu) is: \"<i>ilanot tovot</i>\" (with the feminine adjective). <b>Women</b> also recite this blessing. According to the Ashkenazic custom this is nothing new, for women who wish to may recite blessings over time-bound mitzvot. However, according to the Sephardic custom as well, women may recite the blessing, because the fixed time for Birkat Ha-ilanot does not depend on a mitzva but on the appearance of blossoms on the trees. And this is not considered a time-bound mitzva; rather, natural reality causes it. See Peninei Halakha: Women's Prayer 2:8."}]},{"slug":"10-15-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-15-09/","title_he":"ט – ברכת הודאת גשמים","title_en":"The Blessing of Thanksgiving for Rain","body_en":"<p>The descent of rain from the heavens is one of the tangible expressions of the abundance of life that the Holy One, blessed be He, bestows upon the world, for the lives of man, animal and plant depend on rain. Therefore, throughout the winter, in the second blessing of the Amida prayer we praise God for the rain that He brings down to the world, and in the ninth blessing we ask that He bring down for us rains of blessing.</p><p>In a year in which the rains were withheld, the Sages instituted that if afterward they began to fall, everyone should recite a blessing of thanksgiving, each person according to what is appropriate for him: one who has a field of which he is the sole owner recites Sheheḥeyanu. And one who has a field in partnership with another, or who is married and his wife is a partner with him in his property, as is customary, recites Ha-tov Ve-hametiv.</p><p>One who has no field says a general blessing of thanksgiving based on the wording of Nishmat: \"We give thanks to You, Lord our God and God of our fathers, for each and every drop that You have brought down for us. Were our mouths as full of song as the sea, and our tongues of joyous song as the multitude of its waves, and our lips of praise as the expanse of the firmament, and our eyes shining like the sun and the moon, and our hands spread out like the eagles of the heavens, and our feet swift as hinds – we could not sufficiently thank You, Lord our God and God of our fathers, and bless Your name, our King, for even one of the thousand thousands of thousands, and myriads of myriads of times, of the favors, miracles and wonders that You performed for our fathers and for us. Of old You redeemed us from Egypt, Lord our God, and from the house of bondage You delivered us; in famine You fed us and in plenty You sustained us; from the sword You saved us and from pestilence You rescued us, and from many severe and lasting illnesses You raised us up. Until now Your compassion has helped us and Your kindness has not forsaken us. Do not abandon us, Lord our God, forever. Therefore, the limbs that You have apportioned in us, and the spirit and soul that You breathed into our nostrils, and the tongue that You placed in our mouth – they shall thank and bless Your name, our King. Blessed are You, Lord, God of abundant thanksgivings\" (this is the Ashkenazic wording; in the Sephardic wording there are slight changes).</p><p>Some maintain that even those who have a field must, in addition to the blessing of Sheheḥeyanu or Ha-tov Ve-hametiv, also say the blessing of Modim (Ra'ah and Ritva). However, many maintain that since they must recite their own personal blessing of thanksgiving, they may no longer recite Modim (Rif; Rambam; SA 221:2). Field owners who wish to be meticulous should say the blessing of Modim without God's name and kingship.</p><p>One does not say the blessing of thanksgiving over a small amount of rain, but only after the drops \"go out like a bridegroom toward a bride\" (Berakhot 59b). That is, when the rainwater gathers on the ground, and as a result of the rain falling and striking the water, bubbles and droplets of a sort rise upward, until they look like the dance of a bridegroom and bride (SA 221:1). And this applies even though not enough rain has yet fallen to saturate the ground and fill the water reservoirs.</p><p>If after they recited the blessing over the rain, the rains stopped again, and they again prayed for rain, and once more rains of blessing fell for them, they should recite the blessing of thanksgiving over the rain again as the first time.<sup>6</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"Some say that in the Land of Israel, which needs rain, one recites the blessing over its falling even if it was not withheld (Halakhot Ketanot 1:190; Ma'amar Mordekhai). However, from SA 221:1 it is implied that one recites the blessing only if people suffered from its being withheld. So too wrote the Levush and Pri Megadim (and see BHL, s.v. \"im\"). Some were in doubt whether in our times, when a comprehensive solution has been found for the water supply through desalination of seawater, one who has no field need not recite the blessing over the falling of rain. In practice, even today, there is still great benefit from the falling of rain, and people customarily suffer from its being withheld; therefore, the Sages' enactment to recite the blessing should not be abolished."}]},{"slug":"10-15-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-15-10/","title_he":"י – קשת בענן","title_en":"A Rainbow in the Cloud","body_en":"<p>One who sees the rainbow in the cloud recites: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who remembers the covenant, is faithful to His covenant, and keeps His word\" (<i>zokher ha-brit ve-ne'eman bi-vrito ve-kayam be-ma'amaro</i>). Each time one sees a rainbow, one must recite the blessing anew upon seeing it, even though thirty days have not passed since one last saw it, because every appearance of a rainbow is new, since the previous rainbow has passed away (Birkei Yosef; MB 229:2).</p><p>The special wording of the blessing refers to the covenant that God made with Noaḥ and his descendants after him, that there would never again be a flood upon the earth, as it is stated (Bereishit 9:12-16): \"And God said: This is the sign of the covenant that I give between Me and you and every living creature that is with you, for everlasting generations. I have set My bow in the cloud, and it shall be a sign of the covenant between Me and the earth. And it shall come to pass, when I bring clouds over the earth, that the bow shall be seen in the cloud. And I will remember My covenant, which is between Me and you and every living creature of all flesh, and the waters shall no longer become a flood to destroy all flesh. And the bow shall be in the cloud, and I will see it, to remember the everlasting covenant between God and every living creature of all flesh that is upon the earth.\"</p><p>The Sages said (Ḥagiga 16a) that it is not proper to gaze at the rainbow, because the rainbow reveals the glory and the covenant of God, and whoever gazes at it a great deal has no regard for the honor of his Maker. But a brief look in order to recite the blessing is certainly permitted. Some say that since it is not proper to gaze at the rainbow, one who sees it should recite the blessing, but it is not proper to tell one's friend that the rainbow is visible in the sky (MB 229:1). Others say the opposite – that, on the contrary, it is proper to tell one's friend to see the rainbow, for this is its purpose: to remind human beings that by virtue of the covenant God sustains the world, and so they will be roused to repentance (Berit Kehuna, ma'arekhet kuf, 3). And this is the practice.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-15-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-15-11/","title_he":"יא – ברכת החמה","title_en":"Birkat Ha-ḥama (the Blessing over the Sun)","body_en":"<p>One who sees the sun at its cycle (<i>tekufa</i>), when all seven planets return to their place as they were when they were suspended in the heavens at the creation of the world, recites \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who performs the work of creation.\" This event occurs once every great cycle of the sun, that is, once every twenty-eight years.</p><p>The time of Birkat Ha-ḥama is always on Wednesday morning, as the day of the sun's creation. The previous time was on 14 Nisan 5769 (2009), and the time of the next Birkat Ha-ḥama will be on 23 Nisan 5797 (2037).</p><p>The time of the blessing is in the morning, as early as possible, for the zealous hasten to perform mitzvot. Since this is a rare blessing, many wish to fulfill it in an enhanced manner, and they rise for the vatikin prayer (prayer timed to sunrise), and immediately after the conclusion of the prayer they go outside in a large assembly and recite Birkat Ha-ḥama. Some have the custom to add further psalms of praise for the creation of the luminaries, such as \"The heavens declare the glory of God\" (Tehillim 19), \"Halleluya, praise the Lord from the heavens\" (Tehillim 148), and Hallel Ha-gadol (Tehillim 136).</p><p>One who did not recite the blessing immediately in the morning may, according to many, in a pressing situation recite the blessing until midday (Eliya Rabba; Dagul Me-revava; Arukh Ha-shulḥan; MB 229:8). Some say that one may recite the blessing only until three hours (MA), and some, because of the doubt, were concerned for their opinion and held that one should not recite the blessing with God's name and kingship after three hours (Ḥatam Sofer OḤ 56; Ben Ish Ḥai, Ekev 19).</p><p>When the sky is covered with clouds and the sun is not visible at all, one should not recite Birkat Ha-ḥama, even though there is light from it (Ḥatam Sofer OḤ 56).</p><p>Women's law is like men's, for it is not time that causes the obligation of the blessing but rather the unique position of the sun, and so many women have practiced. Some maintain that women do not customarily recite the blessing, and in their opinion, a woman who wishes to merit the blessing should hear it from the sheliaḥ tzibbur (prayer leader) (see Yeḥaveh Da'at vol. 4, 18:6).</p><p>Birkat Ha-levana (the blessing over the moon), which men recite every month, is explained in Peninei Halakha: Zemanim (1:16-18).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-15-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-15-12/","title_he":"יב – בריות יפות ואילנות","title_en":"Beautiful Creatures and Trees","body_en":"<p>One who sees especially handsome or excellent animals, and likewise one who sees especially beautiful or fine trees, and likewise one who sees an especially handsome person, Jew or gentile, recites: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who has such in His world\" (<i>she-kakha lo be-olamo</i>) (Berakhot 58b). People are often accustomed to marveling at creatures that are especially beautiful, strong or large, and some even hold contests of beauty or strength between certain creatures (people and animals). And although we do not worship beauty or strength or size as the Greeks worshiped them, we nevertheless do not ignore their value. Therefore, the Sages instituted that one who sees a creature whose beauty, size or strength people customarily marvel at should say a blessing of praise to God, thereby emphasizing that everything comes from Him, may He be blessed. And from the fact that the same blessing is recited both over animals and over a person, one can learn that we do not regard beauty as a uniquely human virtue.<sup>7</sup></p><p>And although the Sages instituted this blessing as an obligation, many have the custom not to recite it, because it is often difficult for the viewer to decide whether the creature before him is beautiful enough to recite the blessing over it. But according to the letter of the law, it is proper for a person not to be overly concerned, for the Sages placed the decision in his hands, and whenever he sees a creature that is especially handsome – he should recite the blessing.</p><p>There are two kinds of handsome creatures. The common kind is creatures that are considered especially beautiful or excellent compared to others of their species, such as a horse that is especially handsome and strong, much more so than others of its species; one who discerns this – recites the blessing. But one who is not accustomed to observing horses – should not recite the blessing, since he cannot discern the uniqueness of this horse. And if, after he is told that this horse is especially handsome and strong, he discerns it and is impressed – he recites the blessing. Likewise, one who sees a cow that yields more milk than all the other cows – recites the blessing. And so too regarding other animals, and so too regarding other trees.</p><p>The second kind is species that are considered especially beautiful compared to other species, to the point that people go to see them, such as the beautiful fish in the Gulf of Eilat, which are considered especially beautiful compared to familiar fish; likewise, a large parrot with dazzling colors, which is considered beautiful compared to other birds; and likewise species of giant trees that are considered impressive compared to other trees. Anyone who sees one of these species must recite the blessing.</p><p>Just as one recites the blessing over animals, so one recites the blessing upon seeing a person who is especially handsome, tall or strong, or an athlete with especially outstanding achievements, such as a local champion or an Olympic medalist. It seems that if the special beauty was produced by plastic surgery, or the special strength by the use of steroids, since it is not natural, one should not recite the blessing over it. Although in the past men also recited the blessing over especially beautiful women, today, for reasons of modesty, this is not accepted or proper (see Yerushalmi Berakhot ch. 1, end of halakha 1, and Harḥavot).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":7,"en":"When beauty comes into collision and contradiction with good character traits, it is considered falsehood and vanity, as it is stated (Mishlei 31:30): \"Grace is false and beauty is vain; a woman who fears the Lord, she shall be praised.\" And when beauty reflects the beautiful spiritual inner self of a person, it is praiseworthy, and it is the beauty that the Torah notes regarding the Matriarchs. But usually there is no connection between beauty and inner character, and there are very beautiful people with corrupt character traits, and there are ugly people with good character traits. When the world is repaired, unity will be created between the world of the spirit and the world of matter, and then inner beauty will be expressed outwardly in beautiful appearance."}]},{"slug":"10-15-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-15-13/","title_he":"יג – המשך ההלכה למעשה","title_en":"The Practical Halakha, Continued","body_en":"<p>Over each and every creature that is especially beautiful one recites a separate blessing: if one saw an especially handsome person – he recites the blessing; and if afterward he saw an especially handsome cat – he recites the blessing again; and if afterward he sees an especially handsome dog or horse – he recites the blessing again; and so for every animal and fish. Likewise, if he sees an especially beautiful tree – he recites the blessing; and if afterward he sees an especially beautiful tree of another kind – he recites the blessing again. Even if he sees all of them in one day, he recites a separate blessing over each and every one of them. But if he sees two species together, one blessing covers both of them.</p><p>The poskim disagree as to whether, when one sees the same creature after thirty days, one recites the blessing over it again. In practice, over the same creature one never recites the blessing again; but if after thirty days one sees another creature of the same kind, and in some respect the creature he sees is even more handsome than the creature he saw the previous time, he recites the blessing again. And since beauty is complex, one can say that whenever one sees another especially handsome creature, which is not clearly considered less beautiful than what he saw in the past, it can be determined with certainty that in some respect it is more handsome than the former, and therefore, if thirty days have passed since the previous seeing, he recites the blessing again. That is, over the same horse over which he already recited the blessing he will never recite it again, but if after thirty days he sees another especially handsome horse, since it differs somewhat in appearance and is not considered less handsome than the previous one – he recites the blessing. The same law applies to a person: over the same especially handsome person he should not recite the blessing again, but if after thirty days he sees another handsome person who is not less handsome than the previous one, since his beauty is somewhat different – he recites the blessing. And the same law applies to each and every creature.<sup>8</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"Because of the doubt as to when a creature is considered especially handsome, many have the custom not to recite this blessing (see Ḥayei Adam 63:1; Sha'ar Ha-tziyun 225:33). But no posek has arisen to abolish this blessing from the world. Therefore, whenever one knows that the creature before him is especially handsome – he must recite the blessing. But a person who, even after being told that this horse is especially handsome and has even won a horse competition, does not discern its beauty, should not recite the blessing, because even though he sees it, he nevertheless does not see its special beauty. According to many poskim, if one has not seen the handsome creature for thirty days, he recites the blessing over it again, as with the other blessings over sights (Meiri; Rosh; Radbaz; Rema; and others). According to the Ra'avad, since there is not so much novelty and wonder in the sight of a handsome creature, one recites the blessing over it only once, and even over another of the same kind one does not recite the blessing unless it is more handsome than the previous one. So too wrote the Ra'ah, Ritva, Rabbeinu Manoaḥ, and SA 225:9-10. Some say that all this applies specifically within thirty days, but if after thirty days one sees another handsome creature of the same kind – he recites the blessing (Sefer Ha-batim; Nahar Shalom). Most poskim explain the Ra'avad's position to mean that even over another creature and after thirty days, only if it is more handsome than the first – does one recite the blessing (SA 225:9-10; MB 29-30). However, it seems that their words can be interpreted to mean that whenever the second creature is more handsome in some respect – one recites the blessing. All the more so is this the law according to all the Rishonim and Aḥaronim who did not accept the words of the Ra'avad. Furthermore, the Ra'avad's words are based on the fact that there is no novelty in the second seeing of another creature of the same kind; but if it is very handsome, and in fact one is impressed upon seeing it, it is reasonable that even according to the Ra'avad one recites the blessing. When one does not recite the blessing because of doubt, the MB (32) wrote that it is good to say it without God's name and kingship.<br>According to Halakhot Ketanot (vol. 1, 60:265), one should not praise God over creatures that were born through the prohibition of kilayim (forbidden mixtures of species). According to She'elat Ya'avetz (1:63), since afterward it is permitted to use the tree or the animal that was produced through a prohibition, one recites the blessing over them. In practice, when it is known with certainty that the creature was produced through a prohibition, because of the doubt one should be stringent and not recite the blessing over it. But when there is no such certain knowledge, one should recite the blessing."}]},{"slug":"10-15-14","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-15-14/","title_he":"יד – משנה הבריות","title_en":"Who Varies the Creatures (Meshaneh Ha-briyot)","body_en":"<p>This world is not perfect; there are various natural disasters in it, earthquakes, droughts, cold and heat. Man too is not perfect, and at times a person is born with a defect, extremely unusual, and the sorrow over this is great. Nevertheless, we must strengthen ourselves in faith and know that God created the world lacking, so that we would need to cope with difficulties and thereby merit to reveal the powers hidden within us and to be partners in the repair of the world. Therefore, even at a time of disaster whose meaning we do not understand, we were commanded to recite \"the true Judge\" (<i>Dayan Ha-emet</i>). Likewise, although we do not understand why there are defects and blemishes in the world, we must believe that the Creator, who knows all secrets, conducts His world properly and for the good, and therefore the Sages instituted the blessing upon seeing an unusual person: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who varies the creatures\" (<i>meshaneh ha-briyot</i>).</p><p>One recites the blessing only over a great difference, for example: over one born without a hand or a foot; or one who because of his great height cannot stand upright and his stature is bent; or one who is very much a dwarf; or one whose hairs are all stuck to one another; or Siamese twins, who are joined to one another from birth; or one whose arms are very short relative to his body; and anything similar. One likewise recites the blessing over an animal born with an extremely unusual defect. Over each and every kind of difference one recites a separate blessing. However, if one saw two unusual people together, one blessing covers both of them.</p><p>Here too the poskim disagree as to whether one recites the blessing again after thirty days over the same difference, and since in cases of doubt regarding blessings we are lenient, one recites the blessing over each kind of difference only once.<sup>9</sup></p><p>One must be very careful not to insult the person with the defect while saying the blessing. In any situation where there is concern that he will be insulted, one should recite the blessing quietly. And if even this might insult him, it is preferable not to recite the blessing at all, because the prohibition against insulting and causing pain to a person – and all the more so a suffering person – is greater than the mitzva to recite these blessings.</p><p>Over one who became defective and unusual as a result of an accident or illness, one does not recite \"who varies the creatures.\" If one shares in his sorrow, he should recite the first time he sees him: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, the true Judge\" (SA 225:9).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"It seems that in this case, even if one sees another person somewhat more unusual but with the same kind of difference, he should not recite the blessing. And although in a similar case with handsome creatures one recites the blessing, here, where it is not pleasant to gaze and examine whether the difference is greater than the previous one, one should not recite the blessing. And this is somewhat implied by SA 225:9-10. However, if it is clear to him that the difference is greater and this will arouse feelings of sorrow in him – he recites the blessing."}]},{"slug":"10-15-15","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-15-15/","title_he":"טו – ברכות למבקרים בגן חיות – ראיית קוף ופיל","title_en":"Blessings for Visitors to a Zoo – Seeing a Monkey and an Elephant","body_en":"<p>Visitors to a zoo have the merit of reciting \"who varies the creatures\" twice. For anyone who sees a monkey or an elephant recites: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who varies the creatures\" (Berakhot 58b; SA 225:8). However, if one sees them together, one blessing covers both of them. But if they are in different places, one must recite a separate blessing upon seeing each of them.</p><p>The Sages instituted the blessing specifically over the elephant and the monkey because their appearance arouses special amazement, more than other animals, for although they are animals, they resemble man to some degree. The monkey resembles man in the shape of its body and in the way it uses its hands. And the elephant is exceptional among animals in that its skin is smooth without hair, and it uses its trunk like a hand (Meiri, Berakhot 58b).</p><p>Whenever one has not seen them for thirty days, when he sees them again he recites the blessing again. Although regarding other handsome and unusual creatures the custom is to recite the blessing only upon seeing them for the first time, because it is not so clear whether they are sufficiently unusual or beautiful, here, where it is clear that the Sages instituted the blessing over the elephant and the monkey, if thirty days have passed in which one did not see them – he recites the blessing again.</p><p>Another blessing pertains to a visitor to a zoo: when he sees a parrot with dazzling colors, or another especially beautiful animal – he recites \"who has such in His world.\" And since the standard of beauty is not clear enough, and there may be other beautiful animals there, he should recite the blessing over one beautiful species and intend with his blessing to cover all the other beautiful animals in the zoo. And if thirty days pass until he sees them again – he recites the blessing again.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-15-16","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-15-16/","title_he":"טז – עבודה זרה ובתי גויים רשעים","title_en":"Idolatry and the Houses of Wicked Gentiles","body_en":"<p>When a Jew encounters idolatry on his way, he is entirely filled with shock at the magnitude of the sin and error of its worshippers, and a question arises in his heart: how does God let them sin so much? But he must settle his mind and understand that the Holy One, blessed be He, conducts the world with patience, and allows human beings to arrive at the truth by themselves, without miraculous intervention. And so, in a natural way, through the experience and understanding of human beings, the Holy One, blessed be He, turns circumstances until one error after another is eliminated from the world. In this way, in a gradual process in which human beings are partners, the world will arrive at complete faith. If they choose the good, the process will be short, and if they err greatly, the process will be long and painful. In order to fix this knowledge in our hearts, the Sages instituted a blessing upon seeing idolatry, and a blessing upon seeing the place from which it was uprooted.</p><p>One who sees idolatry recites: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who has granted patience to those who transgress His will\" (<i>she-natan erekh apayim le-overei retzono</i>) (Berakhot 57b; SA 224:1). Specifically when he sees the idolatry itself, that is, the statue or the altar – he recites the blessing; but over the building in which it is located he should not recite the blessing (Baḥ. Over a church and a cross, too, one does not recite the blessing; see Harḥavot).</p><p>If one saw the same idolatry again within thirty days – he should not recite the blessing. And if thirty days have passed – he recites the blessing. And even if thirty days have not passed, if he saw a different idolatry, even of the same kind – he recites the blessing.</p><p>Those who live among idol worshippers, since they are accustomed to seeing their statues, do not recite the blessing upon seeing them. And even if in fact they have not seen them for thirty days, since seeing them is considered routine for them and holds nothing new – they should not recite the blessing (Rema 224:1; Eliya Rabba; MB 3). But if they see statues of another religion with which they are not familiar – they recite the blessing.</p><p>One who sees a place from which idolatry was uprooted recites: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who uprooted idolatry from our land.\" And outside the Land of Israel he concludes: \"from this place.\" After the blessing he says: \"Just as You uprooted it from this place, so may You uproot it from all places, and turn the hearts of their worshippers to worship You.\" And even if it was uprooted from one place and moved to another place, over the place where it was abolished he recites the blessing over its abolition, and over the place where it exists he recites \"who has granted patience to those who transgress His will\" (SA and Rema 224:2). It is fitting for tour guides to include sites where there were idolatrous cults, and to describe to the hikers how in the past their worshippers were certain that they would continue to worship them forever, and in the end, through the influence of the faith of Israel, this idolatry was abolished from the world; and afterward everyone should recite the blessing.</p><p>One who sees the stately houses of wicked gentiles, or their courts and seats of government, says: \"The Lord will tear down (<i>yissaḥ</i>, will destroy) the house of the proud\" (Mishlei 15:25). He likewise says this when he sees a building in which idolatry is worshipped without seeing the statue itself, and he likewise says it when he sees a mosque of wicked people who hate Israel. And when he sees them in their ruin, he says: \"God of vengeance, Lord, God of vengeance, appear!\" (Tehillim 94:1) (SA 224:11; MB 15).<sup>10</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"The Sages further instituted three special blessings over the destruction of the wicked kingdom of Babylonia, which destroyed our Holy Temple. One who sees their royal city that was destroyed recites: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who destroyed wicked Babylon.\" One who sees the house of Nevukhadnetzar recites: \"Blessed … who destroyed the house of the wicked Nevukhadnetzar.\" One who sees a place where any animal that passes over it cannot move unless some of the dust of that place is put on it – which is a sign of the fulfillment of the punishment \"I will sweep it with the broom of destruction\" (Yeshayahu 14:23), that its soil will diminish – recites: \"Blessed … who says and does, who decrees and fulfills\" (Berakhot 57b; SA 224:3-4). Today only the site of their royal city is known (Kaf Ha-ḥayim 224:10).<br>R. Akiva Eiger wrote, based on the Maharsha, that one who himself abolishes idolatry recites: \"who has sanctified us with His commandments and commanded us to uproot idolatry from our land.\" Included in the prohibition to keep away from idolatry is also the prohibition to visit churches, as it is stated (Vayikra 19:4): \"Do not turn to the idols,\" and Ḥazal learned from this that it is forbidden to look at pictures connected with idolatry (Shabbat 149a; SA YD 142:15)."}]},{"slug":"10-15-17","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-15-17/","title_he":"יז – חכם הרזים","title_en":"Who Knows All Secrets (Ḥakham Ha-razim)","body_en":"<p>One who sees sixty myriads (600,000) of Israel gathered in one place in the Land of Israel recites: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who knows all secrets\" (<i>ḥakham ha-razim</i>). In contrast, one who sees sixty myriads of wicked gentiles, for example when they gather for a demonstration of hatred or for idolatry, even outside the Land, says: \"Your mother shall be utterly ashamed; she who bore you shall be disgraced; behold, the last of the nations shall be a wilderness, a dry land and a desert\" (Yirmiyahu 50:12; Berakhot 58a; Rambam, Hilkhot Berakhot 10:11; SA 224:5).</p><p>One of the great wonders of Israel is that they are one people despite the enormous differences between individuals and between the tribes. There are differences in height, in appearance, in strength and in health, and even more than this, there are deep differences in the realms of the spirit. Some are more talented and some less; some have a general, broad perception, and some have a detailed, precise perception; some are intellectual and some emotional; some are patient and some zealous; some are sociable and some solitary. And although these differences often create disagreements and great tensions, despite all this, precisely by virtue of the differences, all of them together create a wondrous, harmonious system of a vibrant and creative people. And for this we recite \"who knows all secrets,\" for He is the One who knows how, out of all the different talents He created, the people of Israel ultimately unite, to receive the Torah and to repair the world under the kingship of the Almighty.</p><p>Among other nations too, different people unite and create great cultures; however, these are cultures whose scope is limited. No nation has succeeded in surviving beyond a certain period, because in the end the particular forces within it prevailed, the nation disintegrated, and the individuals who were scattered united again around another temporary culture. Therefore one does not recite \"who knows all secrets\" over them. And if they are united around evil goals, not only does one not recite the blessing over them, but one must even express protest against their wickedness and note that their end is destruction. In contrast, the people of Israel, despite the great differences between its various components and the sharp disputes between them, does not disintegrate, because this is how we were created by the One who knows all secrets: the different forces ultimately unite into one system with a vital and creative power like no other.</p><p>However, only in the Land of Israel is the unifying power revealed, as it is stated (II Shmuel 7:23): \"And who is like Your people Israel, one nation in the land,\" and therefore only in the Land of Israel, when one sees sixty myriads of Jews, does one recite \"who knows all secrets\" (Olat Re'iya vol. 1, p. 387, based on the Rambam, R. Manoaḥ and Eliya Rabba).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-15-18","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-15-18/","title_he":"יח – על ראיית חכמי ישראל וחכמי אומות העולם","title_en":"On Seeing the Sages of Israel and the Sages of the Nations of the World","body_en":"<p>One who sees one of the sages of Israel says: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who has imparted of His wisdom to those who fear Him\" (<i>she-ḥalak me-ḥokhmato li-re'av</i>). And one who sees one of the sages of the nations of the world says: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who has given of His wisdom to flesh and blood\" (<i>she-natan me-ḥokhmato le-vasar va-dam</i>) (Berakhot 58a; SA 224:6-7).</p><p>In these blessings we praise God for the wisdom He has granted to human beings. For wisdom is the foundation of the world, with which God created the world, as it is stated (Mishlei 3:19): \"The Lord founded the earth with wisdom.\" And He in His great kindness gave of it to human beings, in order to grant them the merit of being His partners in the repair of the world. The great sages have an important part in revealing wisdom and transmitting it, and by their merit the world develops and is elevated; therefore, the Sages instituted a blessing upon seeing them.</p><p>There is a profound difference between the wisdom of the Torah and the external branches of wisdom. For the wisdom of the Torah is Divine wisdom, whose aim is the essential repair of man and the world in the light of faith and morality, and it cannot be properly understood without fear of Heaven and cleaving to God. Whereas the other branches of wisdom deal with understanding the external side of the world and perfecting it – the study of the psyche, society, history, economics, nature and the body. These branches of wisdom have great value, and therefore they are considered a branch of Divine wisdom; however, they are external wisdoms compared to the wisdom of the Torah, and in order to understand them, it is not necessary to be God-fearing.</p><p>The difference between the branches of wisdom finds expression in the wording of the blessing. Over a sage in the external wisdoms one recites: \"who has given of His wisdom to flesh and blood,\" to express the greatness of the gift: although man is flesh and blood, God gave him of His wisdom. And over the sages of Israel one recites: \"who has imparted of His wisdom to those who fear Him.\" The wording \"imparted\" (<i>ḥalak</i>, literally \"shared\") expresses partnership, for the sages of Torah have a share with God in the Torah. And they are called \"those who fear Him,\" because fear of Heaven is a condition for understanding the Torah.</p><p>Regarding a Jewish sage who is wise in external wisdoms, a doubt arose as to how to recite the blessing. In practice, it seems that if he is known as a God-fearing person who engages in Torah, one should recite over him the blessing instituted for the sages of Israel, since his wisdom flows from the Torah and is connected to it, and he is included among \"those who fear Him.\" And if he is not known as such, one should recite over him the blessing instituted for the sages of the nations of the world.</p><p>Today too one must recite these blessings. Admittedly, some maintain that the generations have declined, and we no longer have outstanding sages over whom it is fitting to recite the blessing (Ḥesed La-alafim 224:12). Some say that in our times it is difficult to measure and decide who is a great sage over whom it is fitting to recite the blessing, and because of the doubt one should not recite the blessing (Arukh Ha-shulḥan 224:6). And some say that because of the doubt, these blessings should be said without God's name and kingship (Tzitz Eliezer 14:37).</p><p>However, according to most poskim, today too one must recite the blessing over sages, and so many practice in fact (see Yeḥaveh Da'at 4:16, and Piskei Teshuvot 224, [note] 17). Wisdom is determined according to the standard of the generation: if the sage is considered one of the great sages of the generation, whether in Torah or in science, one recites the blessing over him. And when a person is in doubt whether the sage before him is indeed outstanding in his wisdom, he should recite the blessing without God's name and kingship.</p><p>One who sees the same sage another time, if he has not seen him for more than thirty days – recites the blessing again, as is the law with the other blessings over sights. And over a different sage, even within thirty days – he recites the blessing.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-15-19","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-15-19/","title_he":"יט – ברכה על מלך ישראל ועל מלך גוי","title_en":"A Blessing over a King of Israel and over a Gentile King","body_en":"<p>Seeing a king creates excitement. Here is a person whose origin is dust and whose end is dust, and he too is born of woman like other human beings, yet everyone honors him and he has power and might to rule over his fellow human beings. In order to direct our minds properly, the Sages instituted the saying of a blessing that expresses our faith that kingship belongs to God, and that He is the One who has given of His glory to flesh and blood. This blessing does not take a position as to whether this king is righteous or wicked; rather, it expresses faith that God conducts the world. And at times, because of the sins of the generation, God sets up for them an evil king like Nevukhadnetzar, in order to punish them and arouse them to repentance. Therefore, one recites the blessing over every king, whether righteous or wicked (Responsa Lev Ḥayim 3:55). Through saying the blessing, one's independent spiritual stance toward the king is strengthened, for in the blessing we say that God is the source of authority and honor, and consequently the Torah of God and eternal values take precedence over the king's commands.</p><p>One who sees a king of Israel recites: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who has imparted of His glory to those who fear Him\" (<i>she-ḥalak mi-kevodo li-re'av</i>). And one who sees a king of the gentiles recites: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who has given of His glory to flesh and blood\" (<i>she-natan mi-kevodo le-vasar va-dam</i>). Generally, the kings of the gentiles were proud and cruel, while in contrast the kings of Israel were known as merciful kings (see I Melakhim 20:31), who saw their kingship as a Divine mission to transmit the values of Torah and morality. Admittedly, according to the strict judgment of the prophets of Israel, many of the kings of Israel were considered sinners, but relative to the kings of the gentiles, most of them were considered God-fearing. In any case, in practice, over those wicked kings of Israel it is proper to recite \"who has given of His glory to flesh and blood.\" It seems that if the king is very wicked, far beyond the usual, to the point that according to the law one should rebel against him, whether he is a Jew or a gentile – one should not recite the blessing over him.<sup>11</sup></p><p>The Sages said: It is a mitzva to go and see a king, even if he is one of the kings of the nations of the world, because it is fitting for a person to contemplate the orders of the world and to recognize the power and glory inherent in rulership. This knowledge can serve him as a basis for understanding the values of Torah and faith, whose stature is eternal and incomparably higher. And if he merits, he will discern the difference between the glory of kings in this world and the true glory of the King Messiah (Berakhot 58a; SA 224:9). One who has already seen the king is under no mitzva to see him again; only if he comes with a more distinguished entourage is it a mitzva to see him again (Sefer Ḥasidim; MB 224:13).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"According to most poskim, one must recite the blessing even over wicked kings. So wrote Maharaḥ Palagi in Lev Ḥayim 3:55; Kaf Ha-ḥayim 224:29. However, the Yafeh La-lev 4 wrote, cited in Kaf Ha-ḥayim there, that if he is a Jew, one should recite over him the blessing recited over a gentile king, since he is not included among \"those who fear Him\"; and so wrote Birkat Hashem vol. 4, 3:86. And the Mikhtevei Torah (Gur, no. 64) leans toward this. On the other hand, some maintain that a wicked king should not be honored; see Ḥazon Ovadia, Berakhot pp. 411-412, and Sha'ar Ha-ayin p. 358. The Ḥazon Ovadia concludes that over a wicked king one should recite the blessing without God's name and kingship. However, one can accept their words regarding one who is especially wicked, above and beyond the other evil kings, whose law according to halakha is not law but robbery, and according to the law it is fitting to rebel against him, and consequently it is also not fitting to recognize his kingship and recite the blessing over him. But it is impossible to say that over every king who transgressed the seven Noahide commandments they did not recite the blessing, because all the kings of the past were idol worshippers, and virtually all of them also did not refrain from bloodshed, robbery and adultery, and nevertheless the Sages said to recite the blessing over them. However, regarding the kings of Israel, it is reasonable that the strictness is greater, for they are required to be righteous and to observe the mitzvot specific to a king: that every king have a Torah scroll that he takes with him everywhere, that his heart not be lifted above his brethren, and that he not multiply for himself wives, silver and horses; and also the wording of the blessing over them is \"to those who fear Him.\" Thus, if they do not observe Torah and mitzvot, there is room not to recite the blessing over them."}]},{"slug":"10-15-20","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-15-20/","title_he":"כ – ברכת המלכים בזמן הזה","title_en":"The Blessing over Kings in Our Times","body_en":"<p>The poskim wrote that whoever is significant in government and leadership like a king, and executes by judgment, and no one can change his words, from yes to no or from no to yes, one recites over him the blessing over kings (Responsa Ra'avi Av Beit Din 32; Radbaz 1:296; MA; MB 224:12).</p><p>Accordingly, at this time there are almost no kings left over whom one should recite the blessing. For the heads of democratic states depend on parliament and the courts, and their tenure is limited to a number of years. Only in backward countries do there remain a few rulers with the status of kings, upon seeing whom one should recite the blessing. However, if they are very wicked, such that according to the law one should rebel against them, we have already learned that one should not recite the blessing over them.</p><p>Admittedly, some maintain that one must recite the blessing over heads of state in our times as well, and whenever thirty days have passed, one should recite the blessing again upon seeing them. For the essence of kingship and honor depends on the authority to lead the people. In the past this was expressed in the authority to execute, and today in the authority to lead. Even today this authority indirectly affects human life. For example, a national leader determines economic policy and the allocation of resources, and thereby determines who will have a livelihood and which patients will be treated. It is also within his authority to initiate a war and expand it, usually in consultation with parliament, and in an emergency by personal decision. Admittedly, another condition is mentioned in the poskim, that the king be dressed in his royal garments, but when he disguises himself as one of the people – one should not recite the blessing over him (Responsa Nivḥar Mi-kesef 3). However, today honor is not expressed in special garments; it is a fact that the President of the United States, who wears a suit, is honored more than an African king dressed in garments of embroidery and gold. Rather, the rule is that whenever the president appears in his dignity, with his guards and aides according to the rules of protocol, one recites the blessing over him. But when he is on vacation or in the bosom of his family, one does not recite the blessing upon seeing him. And so several rabbis practiced, reciting the blessing over the presidents of the United States and France, or over the Prime Minister of England. Some recited the blessing over the monarchs of England, for although they have almost no powers, their honor is very great, and their royal dynasty has continued for hundreds of years without interruption.</p><p>However, in practice it seems that one should not recite the blessing over the leaders of democratic states, because they are not considered kings. For this was precisely the aim of the democratic states – to abolish the enormous power of kingship. For this purpose they waged revolutions and wars, executed kings and founded a democratic system of government, according to which the people choose their leaders. And in order to limit and balance the power of the elected leaders, so that they would not over time annex to themselves additional governmental powers that resemble monarchic rule to some degree, they founded a strong parliament and an independent judicial system, and limited the length of the leaders' rule.<sup>12</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":12,"en":"Several Aḥaronim wrote that since heads of state hold powers of pardon, there remains in their hands yet another royal power – that of one who can put to death or keep alive. So too wrote Responsa Afarkesta De-anya 32, and Yeḥaveh Da'at 2:28. And in Shevet Ha-levi 1:35 he wrote that one recites the blessing even over a king or queen who has no authority to pardon without consultation, since they are given honor like no other. Rabbi Zoldan wrote similarly in \"Malkhut Yehuda Ve-Yisrael,\" pp. 109ff. However, as I wrote, it seems that the view of those who maintain that one should not recite the blessing is correct, for how, after all the wars and revolutions against monarchic rule, which continued for hundreds of years and were ultimately crowned with success in the abolition of the monarchy and the establishment of democratic government, can we say that nothing has changed, and the leaders of democratic states are still considered kings and one must recite the blessing over them? So too wrote Responsa Kiryat Ḥana David 2:36, and Revavot Efrayim 3:540 (regarding the President of the United States, who is elected for only four years), and Sha'ar Ha-ayin no. 29 in the name of Avnei Yashfeh 47, and other poskim."}]},{"slug":"10-15-21","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-15-21/","title_he":"כא – קברי ישראל","title_en":"Jewish Graves","body_en":"<p>Among the sights that shake the soul is the sight of graves, which arouses in a person thoughts of his mortality and the shortness of his days. In order to order one's thoughts and direct them properly, the Sages instituted that one who sees Jewish graves recites: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who formed you in judgment, nourished you in judgment, sustained you in judgment, gave you life in judgment and gathered you in judgment, and knows the number of all of you, and will in the future revive you in judgment for the life of the World to Come. Blessed are You, Lord, who revives the dead.\"<sup>13</sup> After the blessing, some have the custom to continue and say from the wording of the Amida prayer: \"You are mighty forever,\" etc., until \"and You are faithful to revive the dead,\" without the conclusion of the blessing.</p><p>One says the blessing only when there are at least two graves, since the wording of the blessing refers to seeing a number of graves (MB 224:16).</p><p>One who sees graves of the wicked and of idol worshippers says (Yirmiyahu 50:12): \"Your mother shall be utterly ashamed; she who bore you shall be disgraced; behold, the last of the nations shall be a wilderness, a dry land and a desert\" (SA 224:12).</p><p>One who has already seen these graves within thirty days – should not recite the blessing again upon seeing them. But if he saw that another grave had been added in the cemetery, or he saw a different cemetery, even if thirty days have not passed – he recites the blessing. One who intends to visit several cemeteries in one day should, when he recites the blessing at the first cemetery, have in mind the other cemeteries he will see that day. And if on the next day he arrives at another cemetery, he recites the blessing again, for a blessing of one day cannot be effective for another day (Birkat Hashem vol. 4, 4:42-43).</p><p>A person who is about to enter a cemetery should preferably recite the blessing at the entrance of the cemetery, so that the blessing guides his thoughts and feelings in the cemetery. And if he did not recite the blessing at first, as long as he still sees the graves – he is obligated to recite the blessing. Even one who sees the graves from afar, if he sees them well and contemplates them – must recite the blessing (Arukh Ha-shulḥan 224:8). Those traveling on the roads on their way, who pass by graves while traveling, if they contemplate the graves and think about the dead – recite the blessing. And all the other travelers who do not contemplate them need not recite the blessing. And if afterward, within thirty days, they enter that same cemetery that they saw inadvertently from the road – they recite the blessing.<sup>14</sup></p><p>Even one who sees a cemetery on Shabbat or Yom Tov must recite the blessing. And although Tziduk Ha-din (the prayer of acceptance of judgment) is not said on them, this blessing is not considered Tziduk Ha-din (Responsa Maharia Assad YD 371; Petaḥ Ha-devir 224:14).<sup>15</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":13,"en":"This is the wording of those of Sephardic descent. The Ashkenazic wording is slightly different: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who formed you in judgment, nourished and sustained you in judgment, caused you to die in judgment, knows the number of all of you in judgment, and will in the future revive you and restore you in judgment. Blessed are You, Lord, who revives the dead.\""},{"n":14,"en":"a) Because their first seeing was inadvertent; b) presumably another grave has been added there in the meantime; c) it is reasonable that at first they did not see all the graves from the road. (And see halakha 5.)"},{"n":15,"en":"Some have the custom to visit the graves of tzaddikim in order to cleave to their legacy. In particular, Jews customarily visited the graves of the Patriarchs in Ḥevron, of Raḥel our Matriarch in Beit Leḥem and of Yosef the righteous in Shekhem. It is likewise told of Kalev ben Yefuneh that he went to prostrate himself on the graves of the Patriarchs, so that by their merit he would be saved from the plot of the spies (Sota 34b). And it is explained (Ta'anit 16a) that on fast days, after the prayer, the custom is to go to the cemetery. There are two reasons for this: a) to remember the day of death and be aroused to repentance; b) so that they will ask for mercy on our behalf (see SA OḤ 579:3; 581:4).<br>Some have the custom to turn to the tzaddikim in prayer, and many argue against them that there is concern of idolatry in this, for there is none besides Him, and one should turn in prayer to Him alone. This is the view of the Maharil (Be'er Heitev 581:17), the Maharal of Prague (Netiv Ha-avoda 12), and Ḥayei Adam 138:5, that prayer at the graves of tzaddikim must be directed to the Holy One, blessed be He, alone, and one may ask that by the merit of the tzaddikim, to whose legacy we are connecting, God will hear our prayer. In contrast, the view of many poskim (Pri Megadim 581; Maharam Schick OḤ 293) is that it is permitted even to turn to the souls of the tzaddikim, to whose legacy we are connecting, that they ask for mercy on our behalf before the Holy One, blessed be He. In their view, since we are asking that the tzaddikim pray to the Holy One, blessed be He, for us, it is clear that we acknowledge that we are in God's hands alone. In practice, ideally it is good to be stringent, and one who is lenient to turn to the tzaddikim so that they will entreat God, may He be blessed, on his behalf has something on which to rely."}]},{"slug":"10-15-22","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-15-22/","title_he":"כב – מציב גבול אלמנה","title_en":"Who Establishes the Border of the Widow","body_en":"<p>One who sees Jewish houses in the Land of Israel that have been destroyed recites: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, the true Judge\" (Berakhot 58b; SA 224:10). One should recite the blessing also upon seeing the remains of houses that Jews destroyed and handed over to strangers under pressure from the nations, such as the houses of the communities of the Yamit region and Gush Katif.</p><p>In contrast, the Sages instituted that one who sees Jewish houses in their settled state recites: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who establishes the border of the widow\" (<i>matziv gevul almana</i>) (Berakhot 58b; SA 224:10). The point of this blessing is to thank God for the return of Israel to their land. For after we sinned before Him, we were exiled from our land and became an object of mockery and derision among the nations, until we resembled a widow wandering, broken and forlorn, with no further chance of returning to her border and building her home. And now God has had mercy on us and returned us to our land, to build houses in it and dwell in them in tranquility and security. And the words of the prophet Yeshayahu have begun to be fulfilled in us (54:3-5): \"For you shall spread out to the right and to the left, and your seed shall possess the nations and make the desolate cities inhabited. Fear not, for you shall not be ashamed, and be not confounded, for you shall not be put to shame; for you shall forget the shame of your youth, and the reproach of your widowhood you shall remember no more. For your Maker is your husband, the Lord of hosts is His name, and your Redeemer is the Holy One of Israel; the God of the whole earth shall He be called.\"</p><p>During the years of the harsh exile, when the Jewish community in the Land was in distress, hardship and humiliation, it was not customary to recite this blessing, because it was difficult to define their settlement as stable and there was not much consolation in it. And when Jewish settlement in the Land began to expand, with the departure of the members of the Old Yishuv from the walls of Jerusalem and with the aliya of the Ḥovevei Tziyon, they began to recite this blessing over the new settlements. Thus it is told of Rabbi Shmuel Salant that he recited the blessing over Petaḥ Tikva, and of Rabbi Mordekhai Gimpel Yaffe that he recited the blessing over Yehud.</p><p>The Rif wrote that one who sees Jewish synagogues recites this blessing. Ideally, one should take his position into account and recite the blessing when one sees the synagogue in the settlement. But even one who saw a settlement without its synagogue – recites the blessing (Rabbeinu Ḥananel; Rashi; Rambam; SA 224:10).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-15-23","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-15-23/","title_he":"כג – על איזה ישובים לברך ואימתי","title_en":"Over Which Settlements One Recites the Blessing, and When","body_en":"<p>According to the enactment of the Sages, one should recite \"who establishes the border of the widow\" over every Jewish settlement in the Land of Israel that one sees for the first time, and afterward, whenever one has not seen it for thirty days, one must recite the blessing again (SA 224:10, 13). Seemingly, according to this, one would have to recite the blessing over Tel Aviv, Reḥovot and Netanya as well, and all the settlements near them.</p><p>However, now that we have merited, by the kindness of God, that there are regions of the Land that have been settled for generations with cities and villages, the sorrow of exile has been forgotten from them, and one who sees the Jewish houses in them does not pay attention to the settling of the Land that was accomplished through them, and it is no longer evident that the border of the widow has been established there. And a fundamental rule in the blessings over sights is that one recites the blessing only over something whose sight involves novelty. Therefore, in all the areas properly settled by multitudes of the House of Israel, one does not recite \"who establishes the border of the widow.\"</p><p>But in areas that are not yet properly settled, where further effort is still needed to fulfill the mitzva of settling the Land in them, so that it will be in our hands and not in the hands of another nation, nor desolate – one who sees a settlement in them, even an old one – recites the blessing. Included in these areas are: Judea and Samaria, the Golan, and parts of the Negev and the Galilee. It seems that only one who is somewhat moved upon seeing the settlement for the first time may recite the blessing. Likewise, if he comes a second time and sees that a new neighborhood has been built in the meantime, and he is somewhat moved – he recites the blessing. But if he is not moved at all, he should not recite the blessing, for we have learned that the Sages did not institute the blessings over sights for routine seeing. And regarding an additional visit to the same settlement after thirty days, without a new neighborhood having been built in it, since this does not involve much novelty, only if he is greatly moved by the settling of the Land in them – does he recite the blessing.</p><p>In new settlements in those areas, the excitement is presumably greater, and it is more common that after thirty days have passed one is moved upon seeing them and may recite the blessing. Especially when additional houses have been built there in the meantime, one who sees them must recite the blessing. It also seems that when a new house is dedicated in the new settlements, one must recite \"who establishes the border of the widow.\"</p><p>It seems that in Jerusalem, our holy and glorious city, over whose destruction we mourn and for whose rebuilding we pray, although its settlement is long-established and hundreds of thousands of Jews already live in it, it is proper for one who goes to see a new neighborhood that has been built in it, and is moved by the return of Israel to Jerusalem, to recite the blessing upon seeing it. Even over a number of new buildings, if a ceremony is held in honor of their settlement, it is fitting to recite the blessing over them. For the building of Jerusalem expresses more than anything the establishment of the border of the widow.</p><p>One who is in doubt regarding these laws should say the blessing in the language of Talmud study: \"The Rabbis taught: One who sees Jewish houses in their settled state says – Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who establishes the border of the widow.\" In this way there will be no concern of a blessing in vain, for some maintain (Ya'avetz) that in the course of Talmud study one may say a complete blessing; and on the other hand, one fulfills the obligation of the blessing with this recitation, since God's name and kingship are mentioned in it.<sup>16</sup></p><p>May it be His will that through the establishment of the border of the widow and the thanksgiving for it, the verse will be fulfilled in us: \"And as the bridegroom rejoices over the bride, so shall your God rejoice over you\" (Yeshayahu 62:5). \"And I will betroth you to Me forever; and I will betroth you to Me in righteousness and in justice, and in kindness and in compassion. And I will betroth you to Me in faithfulness, and you shall know the Lord\" (Hoshea 2:21[-22]).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":16,"en":"My teacher and master, Rabbi Tzvi Yehuda Kook, of blessed memory, explained that the basis of this blessing is praise and thanksgiving for the settling of the Land, through which the border of the widow was established. He explained that even the view of the Rif, who holds that one recites the blessing over synagogues, is because they are an extension of the holiness of the Land of Israel (based on Berakhot 8a). And he defined the doubts that are raised regarding this blessing as doubts in faith (Siḥot Ha-Ratziya, Vayikra pp. 288-291, and Olat Re'iya vol. 2, p. 422). According to the basic law, one would have to recite the blessing over every settlement in the Land, and every thirty days. However, when the seeing is routine, there is no longer wonder, and consequently one does not recite the blessing over it – as in the case of those who live in a place of idolatry, who do not recite the blessing even if they have not seen it for thirty days, as explained in Rema 224:1, Eliya Rabba, and MB 3. And when there is some novelty, one recites the blessing only the first time, as explained in SA 225:9, and above, halakha 13, regarding beautiful and unusual creatures.<br>Two factors determine whether this is a routine seeing over which one does not recite the blessing, or a meaningful seeing over which one does recite it: a) The more the houses settle the Land – that is, the more they are in places that still require effort to settle so that the Land will be in our hands and not in the hands of another nation, nor desolate – the greater the obligation to recite the blessing. And when they are in places that are properly settled – one does not recite the blessing. b) Even in places where the houses are significant for the mitzva of settling the Land, if the viewer is impressed by the settling of the Land accomplished through them, he becomes more obligated in the blessing (every thirty days), and if he is slightly impressed, he recites the blessing only the first time. For the blessings over sights depend on the wonder that the seeing arouses (just as one who does not discern the beauty of a handsome creature does not recite the blessing over it).<br>It seems to my humble opinion that even in the settled areas (between Haifa, Ashkelon and Jerusalem), one who goes on a guided tour in order to contemplate the process of Israel's return to its land, and as a result sees the Jewish houses in their settled state and the synagogue at their center – recites the blessing. And even this is on condition that he does not live in that area and has also not been there for thirty days. And on the next guided tour, after thirty days, only if he is greatly moved – does he recite the blessing. It also seems that a Jew who comes from abroad and sees for the first time the large cities of the coastal plain, if he contemplates their settlement and is impressed by the return of Israel to its land – recites the blessing. And it is reasonable that one who sees for the first time a new city built magnificently, even though it is in a settled area, if he is impressed by the power of the settlement in it – recites the blessing.<br>I heard from my teacher and master, Rabbi Shapira, that even when the settlements are under a security threat of bombardment or terror attacks, and even when the government wishes to reach an agreement with the Arabs and hand them over to them, one should recite the blessing upon seeing them. And although the Beit Yosef wrote that one recites the blessing over the houses when they are settled without adversary and without evil mishap, nevertheless the houses are still settled securely relative to our situation in exile under the yoke of foreigners."}]},{"slug":"10-15-24","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-15-24/","title_he":"כד – מקומות שנעשו ניסים לישראל","title_en":"Places Where Miracles Were Performed for Israel","body_en":"<p>One who sees a place where miracles were performed for Israel, or for the majority of Israel, says: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who performed miracles for our fathers in this place.\" The Sages mentioned several places:<br>a) The place where Israel passed through the sea on dry land when the Red Sea was split before them. b) The place where Israel crossed the Jordan on dry ground. c) The crossings of the streams of Arnon – for when Israel approached the Land of Israel and were about to pass through the ravine, the Amorites prepared an ambush for them, and a miracle occurred: the mountains clung to one another and crushed the enemy. d) The descent of Beit Ḥoron, where God cast hailstones from heaven upon the five kings who fought against Yehoshua bin Nun. e) The stone that Og king of Bashan sought to hurl upon Israel. f) The stone on which Moshe our teacher sat with his hands raised to the heavens during the war with Amalek. g) The walls of Yeriḥo, which were swallowed up in their place (Berakhot 54; SA 218:1).</p><p>Whenever one has not seen one of these places for thirty days, one must recite the blessing over it again (SA 218:3). Today we do not know with certainty where these places are, and although we know where the Red Sea, the Jordan and Yeriḥo are, as long as we do not know where the miracle occurred, one cannot recite the blessing (Kaftor Va-feraḥ; BHL, s.v. \"kegon\"). But it is good to say the blessing without God's name and kingship near the presumed place of the miracle (Kaf Ha-ḥayim 218:4).</p><p>One who sees the place of the furnace from which Ḥananya, Mishael and Azarya were saved, or the place of the lions' den from which Daniel was saved, or a place where a miracle was performed for other righteous people, and through that miracle the name of Heaven was sanctified in public, recites: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who performed a miracle for the righteous in this place.\" These places too have been forgotten by us.</p><p>One who sees a place where a king of Israel or a commander of the army of Israel was saved recites: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who performed a miracle for so-and-so in this place\" (Yerushalmi Berakhot 1:1; SA 218:7, and see BHL there).</p><p>One who sees the pillar of salt of Lot's wife recites two blessings:<br>a) \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, the true Judge,\" over her death.<br>b) \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who remembers the righteous,\" over the rescue of Lot by the merit of Avraham (Berakhot 54b; SA 218:8). The place of the pillar of salt is also not known to us.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-15-25","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-15-25/","title_he":"כה – מקומות שנעשו נסים לאדם","title_en":"Places Where Miracles Were Performed for an Individual","body_en":"<p>A person who was in danger, and a miracle was performed for him and he was saved, when he returns and sees the place where the miracle was performed for him, recites: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who performed a miracle for me in this place.\"</p><p>However, there is a question: what is the definition of a miracle? Some say that whoever was in tangible danger of death, for example, he was in a road accident, or fell from a great height, or robbers attacked him – since he was saved, he is obligated to recite the blessing (Beit Yosef in the name of the Rivash). And some say that one recites the blessing only over a real miracle, that is, the rescue was out of the ordinary, for a large majority of those in that danger die, and a miracle was performed for him and he was saved (R. David Abudarham in the name of R. Asher of Lunel).</p><p>In practice, it has been ruled that since in cases of doubt regarding blessings we are lenient, only when the rescue was not in the way of the world should one recite the blessing with God's name and kingship. And in other cases where one was saved from tangible danger of death, one should say: \"Blessed is He who performed a miracle for me in this place,\" without God's name and kingship (SA 218:9).</p><p>And what is a rescue not in the way of the world? A danger in which a large majority of those in it die. For example, a person on whom a large building collapsed, and they dug among the ruins and found him alive; or who fell from a very great height; or at whom several bullets were fired that struck his body; or who was in a very severe road accident – if he was saved, he recites the blessing with God's name and kingship. But if the danger was such that it is not clear that a significant majority of those who enter it die, he recites the blessing without God's name and kingship. And since a person is biased in his own favor, he should not decide by himself whether he was indeed in a danger from which a large majority die; rather, he should ask a Torah scholar, and the scholar, based on the estimate of experts, will rule whether he must recite the blessing with God's name and kingship.<sup>17</sup></p><p>After it has been agreed that the event is a real miracle, when he sees the place of the miracle for the first time, even if thirty days have not passed since the miracle, he recites the blessing. Afterward, if he sees the place after thirty days in which he has not seen it – he recites the blessing again. And if several miracles were performed for him, it is good that he append to the conclusion of the blessing the names of the other places where miracles were performed for him (SA 218:5; Sha'ar Ha-tziyun 12).</p><p>Just as the one for whom the miracle occurred is obligated to recite the blessing, so too his children and grandchildren must recite the blessing as he does when they pass there, if they have not seen the place of the miracle for thirty days. And they recite thus: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who performed a miracle for my father/grandfather in this place\" (SA 218:4). And if he is a rabbi, his primary students must also recite the blessing as he does (ibid. 6). Regarding the great-grandchildren and their descendants, the Rishonim disagree. In practice, descendants who were born by virtue of the miracle – that is, their first ancestor was born after the miracle occurred – as long as they are moved upon seeing the place, must recite the blessing until the end of all generations. And those born before the miracle occurred, from the great-grandchildren of the one for whom the miracle occurred onward – should not recite the blessing (Eliya Rabba; MB 218:16).<sup>18</sup></p><p>The Aḥaronim further wrote that one for whom a miracle was performed and who was saved from danger should donate charity to Talmud Torah schools and yeshivot according to his means, and say: \"I hereby give this to charity, and may it be His will that it be considered in place of the thanksgiving offering that I would have been obligated to bring in the time of the Temple.\" It is fitting to say the passage of the thanksgiving offering. It is also good to volunteer something for the benefit of the public. And every year he should remember the day of the miracle, and increase Torah study, prayer and thanksgiving to God (MB 218:32).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":17,"en":"According to the Meiri and the Rivash (337), even if thieves came upon him and he was saved, he recites the blessing, for he was in mortal danger. And the implication is even a danger from which a large majority are saved. Seemingly their words are puzzling: how can they consider something that is in the natural course of things a miracle (MA)? According to their words, all sick people and women giving birth who recover would need to give thanks for a miracle (Gra). Therefore, it seems that they spoke of a danger in which the percentage of deaths is significant, but presumably does not approach fifty percent (and see BHL 218:9, s.v. \"ve-yesh\").<br>In contrast, the view of R. David Abudarham in the name of R. Asher of Lunel is that one recites the blessing only over a miracle that is out of the ordinary. And since in cases of doubt regarding blessings we are lenient, the practice follows them. However, their words require explanation. Some explained simply that the intent is a miracle that could not have occurred according to the laws of nature; so too wrote Yalkut Yosef 218:4, and Birkat Hashem vol. 4, 6:45. However, their explanation cannot be accepted, for in Berakhot 54a three examples of a miracle are brought: a) one who was attacked by a lion and was saved; b) one who was in the desert and nearly died of thirst, and suddenly a spring gushed forth and he was saved; c) one who was attacked by a wild camel and was saved. And although the second example can be interpreted as a miracle outside the bounds of nature, the two other examples are cases whose danger is great, but the rescue was not outside the bounds of nature. And this is implied by the wording of SA 218:9: \"that it is out of the ordinary course of the world,\" and it did not state that it is a miracle outside the bounds of nature; the implication is that the reference is to a danger from which a large majority die. This, then, is the opinion that, because of doubt regarding blessings, one should follow. And so wrote in practice Ḥayei Adam 65:4, and Nishmat Adam there, Mor U-ketzi'a 218, Ben Ish Ḥai, Ekev 11, MB 32, Sha'ar Ha-tziyun 28, Shevet Ha-levi 7:28. And although some of the poskim I mentioned maintain that whenever there was tangible mortal danger, even if a large majority do not die from it, one recites \"who performed a miracle for me,\" I have taken the middle path."},{"n":18,"en":"According to the basic law, one who sees the person for whom the miracle occurred must also recite the blessing upon seeing him. If he is his father, he recites: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who performed a miracle for my father.\" If he is his rabbi, he concludes \"for my rabbi,\" and if for his grandfather, he concludes \"for my father's father\" (Rema 218:6; MB 21-22; Sha'ar Ha-tziyun 17). However, we have not heard that this is the practice, and apparently seeing the person for whom the miracle occurred is not as moving as seeing the place, and a central foundation of the blessings over sights is that the seeing involves novelty, over which one recites the blessing. It should be added that according to the Ra'avad, Rashba, Ha-mikhtam, and Ha-me'orot in the name of the Ra'avad, this blessing is optional. If so, regarding the blessing over the person for whom the miracle occurred, one can say that only when one is impressed, upon seeing him, by the miracle that was performed for him – does one recite the blessing."}]}]});
